(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-cms-exam-7-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 7 Master Exam — Form D","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 26-year-old tall, thin man has acute dyspnea and sharp left-sided chest pain that worsens with deep breathing. His upright chest radiograph shows a thin, sharp visceral pleural line on the left with no lung markings beyond it. Which examination finding is expected on the left?","choices":["Bronchial breath sounds","Dullness to percussion","Increased tactile fremitus","Hyperresonance to percussion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Percussion over a primary spontaneous pneumothorax is hyperresonant, because air fills the pleural space between the lung and the chest wall. His film confirms that air: a visceral pleural line with no bronchovascular markings beyond it. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 26, 87, 88"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman on a ventilator after a fall suddenly becomes hypotensive at 74/46 mm Hg. Her trachea has shifted to the right, her neck veins are distended and breath sounds are absent on the left. No chest tube kit is readily available on the unit. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Chest radiograph first","Catheter aspiration","Needle decompression","Chest computed tomography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tension pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis, and time is critical. Immediate chest tube placement is ideal, but when a chest tube is not readily available, immediate needle decompression is performed, followed by a moderate or large chest tube and admission. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 33, 39, 64, 66"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man has had sharp, well-localized left chest pain for 3 days after a viral upper respiratory infection. The pain worsens with deep breathing and coughing, and a pleural friction rub (a scratching, creaking sound) disappears when he holds his breath. Myocardial infarction, pneumothorax and pneumonia have been excluded, and his chest radiograph is normal. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Anti-inflammatory drug","Non-rebreather oxygen","Chest tube insertion","Chemical pleurodesis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pleurisy is treated by treating the underlying condition and giving nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. His viral infection is an infectious cause, the dangerous causes are excluded, and the anti-inflammatory drug addresses the parietal pleural inflammation behind his pain. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 10, 13, 14, 41, 45, 53"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman has 1 year of dry cough and exertional dyspnea, bibasilar inspiratory crackles and a restrictive pattern. She has no exposures, medicines, connective tissue disease or sarcoidosis findings, and computed tomography shows fibrosis that does not settle the subtype of idiopathic interstitial pneumonia. What is the next step?","choices":["Lung transplant evaluation","Lung biopsy to define the subtype","Ophthalmology for yearly eye checks","Diagnosis from the scan alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Idiopathic fibrosing interstitial pneumonia may require biopsy for diagnosis, and treatment plan and prognosis depend on the subtype diagnosed. The rule that spares biopsy applies to patients over 65 who meet all four criteria; she is 46 and her scan leaves the subtype open. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 32, 35, 48, 54, 71"},{"q":"Which central lung cancer can be diagnosed with sputum cytology?","choices":["Squamous cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma","Small cell carcinoma","Large cell carcinoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Squamous cell carcinoma arises in the central bronchi, and unlike the other lung cancers it can be caught with sputum testing, even though sputum cytology is otherwise not sensitive. Its bronchial origin is also why cough, wheezing and hemoptysis appear once it obstructs. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 30, 33, 39, 40, 41"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease takes a diuretic for leg swelling. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.42 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 64 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 40 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 139 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 89 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). From a baseline carbon dioxide of 40 mm Hg and bicarbonate of 25 mEq/L, in chronic respiratory acidosis bicarbonate rises 3.5 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg rise in carbon dioxide, and in metabolic alkalosis carbon dioxide rises 0.75 mm Hg per 1 mEq/L rise in bicarbonate. Which acid-base disorders are present?","choices":["Chronic respiratory acidosis with metabolic alkalosis","Metabolic alkalosis with appropriate compensation","Chronic respiratory acidosis with appropriate compensation","Acute respiratory acidosis with metabolic alkalosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chronic compensation predicts bicarbonate = 25 + 3.5 x 2.4 = about 33; hers is 40, higher. Read from the metabolic side, expected carbon dioxide = 40 + 0.75 x 15 = about 51; hers is 64, higher. Both disorders are present and the pH sits at a normal 7.42, the listed pattern for this disease on diuretics. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 29, 24, 34, 53"},{"q":"For which pulmonary embolism risk category is thrombolysis highly controversial?","choices":["Low risk, normotensive","Massive (high risk)","Chronic thromboembolic disease","Submassive (intermediate risk)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Systemic and catheter-directed thrombolysis is the gold standard for massive, hemodynamically unstable embolism, but it is highly controversial in submassive embolism, where rapid right ventricular recovery must be weighed against major bleeding and intracranial hemorrhage risk. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 58, 60, 61"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has had obesity hypoventilation syndrome for many years. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.33 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 66 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 34 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). For respiratory acidosis, bicarbonate is expected to rise 1 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg rise in carbon dioxide when acute and 3.5 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg when chronic, from baselines of 40 mm Hg and 25 mEq/L. Which process accounts for his bicarbonate of 34?","choices":["The lungs retaining carbon dioxide to compensate","The kidneys excreting acid within minutes","The kidneys retaining bicarbonate over days","A second, primary metabolic alkalosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A primary respiratory acidosis is compensated by the kidneys, which retain bicarbonate over hours to days. A chronic rise of 26 mm Hg predicts 25 + 3.5 x 2.6 = about 34, exactly his value, so this is the expected renal compensation. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 19, 23, 24, 34"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with a body mass index of 42 kg/m2 has obesity hypoventilation syndrome, confirmed by exclusion. Her husband has witnessed few apneas, and polysomnography with continuous carbon dioxide monitoring shows sleep-related hypoventilation. Which first-line treatment fits her?","choices":["Bariatric surgery as first therapy","Weight loss plus continuous positive airway pressure","Weight loss plus bilevel positive airway pressure","Tracheostomy with nocturnal ventilation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. First-line therapy for obesity hypoventilation syndrome is a weight loss program with positive airway pressure, and the device follows the overlap: with sleep-related hypoventilation it is bilevel positive airway pressure. Her few witnessed apneas, older age and female sex fit that 10% subgroup. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 33, 30, 32"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with a massive pulmonary embolism has had a blood pressure of 70/40 mmHg for 25 minutes. He also has active bleeding from a duodenal ulcer, with black stools and a hemoglobin of 7.8 g/dL (normal 13.5-17.5 g/dL). Which intervention fits?","choices":["Thrombectomy or embolectomy","Anticoagulation and observation","Thrombolysis after transfusion","Catheter-directed thrombolysis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Massive embolism with sustained hypotension needs immediate advanced intervention, but active bleeding is an absolute contraindication to thrombolytics. Mechanical thrombectomy or surgical embolectomy physically removes the clot without a drug that would worsen his bleeding. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 58, 60"},{"q":"Which treatment for sleep-disordered breathing is itself a risk factor for central sleep apnea?","choices":["Continuous positive airway pressure","Positional therapy with a sleep belt","Oral appliance worn during sleep","Nasal expiratory resistance device"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients using continuous positive airway pressure are among those at risk for central sleep apnea. The same therapy is still the first device to consider in central sleep apnea, with adaptive servo-ventilation if it fails. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 12, 24, 26"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man has 5 days of fever, cough with purulent sputum and right pleuritic chest pain, and a right pleural effusion is tapped. Pleural fluid protein is 4.4 g/dL with serum protein 7.2 g/dL (normal 6.0-8.3 g/dL). Pleural fluid lactate dehydrogenase is 240 units/L with serum lactate dehydrogenase 210 units/L (normal 140-280 units/L). Which is the most likely cause of the effusion?","choices":["Pneumonia","Nephrotic syndrome","Cirrhosis with ascites","Heart failure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Protein ratio 4.4 / 7.2 = 0.61, above 0.5; lactate dehydrogenase ratio 240 / 210 = 1.14, above 0.6; fluid lactate dehydrogenase 240 exceeds two-thirds of 280, about 187. The fluid is an exudate, pneumonia is the most common cause of exudative effusion, and fever with purulent sputum and pleuritic pain is its clue set. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 86, 101, 102, 110, 111, 112"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man settles into bed in a sleep laboratory, and a few minutes later he is drowsy and drifting off into light sleep. Which stage is he in?","choices":["Stage N3","Stage N2","Rapid eye movement sleep","Stage N1"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Stage N1 is the transition from wakefulness: the person is somnolent or drowsy and in light sleep. Minutes after settling into bed and drifting off, he fits this first non-rapid eye movement stage, before the deeper stages N2 and N3. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 28, 27, 6, 7"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man had a traumatic hemothorax that was only partly drained, and a collection of blood remains in his pleural space weeks later. Which complication does this retained blood most directly risk?","choices":["Chylothorax","Empyema","Pneumomediastinum","Tension pneumothorax"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Undrained blood collections serve as a primary nidus for infection, and a retained hemothorax leads directly to empyema, as well as fibrothorax and trapped lung. That is why tube thoracostomy, the essential first step, aims to evacuate the blood. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 63, 74, 75, 104"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman has been breathing rapidly for 30 minutes during a panic attack. She has not vomited, has no diarrhea and takes no medicines. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.54 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 28 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 23 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Her potassium is 3.3 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L). Which process best accounts for her potassium level?","choices":["Severe depletion of total body potassium","Potassium lost through the gut from vomiting","Potassium shifted out of cells as hydrogen ions entered","Potassium shifted into cells as hydrogen ions left them"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A pH of 7.54 with a carbon dioxide of 28 is a respiratory alkalosis from anxiety. In alkalosis hydrogen ions shift out of cells and potassium shifts into cells, resulting in hypokalemia, with no potassium actually lost from the body. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 5, 39"},{"q":"What is the Palla sign on a chest film in pulmonary embolism?","choices":["Central perihilar edema","Focal peripheral oligemia","Enlarged right descending artery","Wedge-shaped pleural opacity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The Palla sign is an enlarged right descending pulmonary artery. With the Westermark sign and Hampton's hump it is a classic chest film clue, highly suggestive of pulmonary embolism but infrequent; most films in embolism are abnormal but nonspecific. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 51, 37"},{"q":"Which chest radiograph finding points to sarcoidosis or silicosis among the interstitial lung diseases?","choices":["Hilar and mediastinal adenopathy","Normal heart, air bronchograms","Pleural plaques at the bases","Pleural-based honeycombing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. On chest radiography, hilar and mediastinal lymphadenopathy points to sarcoidosis or silicosis. Sarcoidosis shows symmetric bilateral hilar and right paratracheal adenopathy, and silicosis can calcify the periphery of the hilar nodes in an eggshell pattern. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 26, 34, 44, 58, 63, 73"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman's recording during a period of rapid eye movements shows irregular, high-frequency waves, and the technician labels it desynchronized sleep. What does that label describe?","choices":["Neurons firing without synchrony","Fatigue of the activating system","Rhythmic waves of quiet rest","Neurons firing in tight unison"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rapid eye movement sleep is also called desynchronized sleep because there is a lack of synchrony in neuron firing when there is brain activity. The tracing is irregular and high frequency, much like an awake, active person's, which fits her period of rapid eye movements. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 29, 22, 19"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has renal tubular acidosis, irritable bowel syndrome with intermittent diarrhea, and glaucoma treated with an oral carbonic anhydrase inhibitor. His roommate found him confused beside an open container of antifreeze. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.23 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 22 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 9 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Serum sodium 140 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 105 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 9 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which part of his history best accounts for this blood gas?","choices":["Intermittent diarrhea","The carbonic anhydrase inhibitor","Ethylene glycol in the antifreeze","Renal tubular acidosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Low pH with a bicarbonate of 9 is a metabolic acidosis, and the gap is 140 - (9 + 105) = 26, well above 12. Ethylene glycol is a toxic alcohol on the raised-gap list. His other conditions all cause a normal-gap acidosis. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 44, 45, 46, 47"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with loud snoring, witnessed apneas and daytime sleepiness is seen for evaluation before elective surgery. He has never been tested for sleep apnea. Which property makes the STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) questionnaire suited to this visit?","choices":["It confirms the diagnosis before surgery","It grades his disease severity","Highest sensitivity of the questionnaires","Highest specificity of the questionnaires"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The questionnaire has been used as a preoperative evaluation tool for people without a known diagnosis of obstructive sleep apnea who may be at risk for perioperative complications, and it has the highest sensitivity among the questionnaires. A highly symptomatic patient in a high-risk setting is where it has value. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 16, 17, 8, 9"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man has severe obstructive sleep apnea and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, with nocturnal hypoxemia. Echocardiography shows a normal left ventricle and an enlarged right ventricle, and his ventilation-perfusion scan is normal. Which World Health Organization pulmonary hypertension group fits?","choices":["Group 1","Group 2","Group 3","Group 4"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lung disease and hypoxia make up Group 3, and both of his conditions are on that list: chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and sleep apnea, with its chronic nocturnal alveolar hypoventilation. Alveolar hypoxia constricts the pulmonary vessels and loads his right ventricle. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 5, 6, 19"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with chronic respiratory acidosis from kyphoscoliosis is intubated, and the ventilator lowers her carbon dioxide from 70 to 40 mm Hg within an hour. Her bicarbonate is 35 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which complication is she now at risk for?","choices":["Cardiac arrhythmias and seizures","Improved oxygen delivery to tissues","A worsening metabolic acidosis","Hyperkalemia from potassium leaving cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In chronic respiratory acidosis the kidneys have raised bicarbonate over days, and it falls slowly. Lowering carbon dioxide rapidly leaves that bicarbonate unopposed, and the alkalosis may cause cardiac arrhythmias, reduced cerebral perfusion and seizures. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 34, 37, 5"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man with chronic kidney failure has fatigue. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.29 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 30 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 14 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Serum sodium 138 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 103 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 14 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L), potassium 4.0 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L). What is his anion gap?","choices":["11","21","25","35"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Anion gap = sodium - (bicarbonate + chloride) = 138 - (14 + 103) = 138 - 117 = 21, above the normal 6 to 12. A raised gap with a low pH and low bicarbonate is a raised-gap metabolic acidosis, and renal failure is on that list. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 7, 26, 45"},{"q":"A 37-year-old woman believes that sleep matters for the brain and nothing else. Which statement about the physiological effects of sleep is accurate?","choices":["Its effects are purely psychological","It affects the muscles alone","It affects the brain alone","It affects brain and body systems"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sleep has two major types of physiological effects: effects on the nervous system and effects on the other functional systems of the body. Its functions span both, from neural maturation and cognition to conservation of metabolic energy, so its importance is not limited to the brain. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 17, 12, 10"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman who still smokes and has a 50 pack-year history has had 6 weeks of hemoptysis and has lost 4 kg. She saw an advertisement and asks to enroll in a lung cancer screening program. Which plan should be avoided?","choices":["Enrolling her in annual screening","Metabolic panel with calcium","Contrast chest computed tomography","Biopsy of any lesion found"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Screening is for high-risk individuals without symptoms or a known nodule. Hemoptysis and weight loss are symptoms that should be worked up for possible lung cancer now, with computed tomography with contrast of the chest and upper abdomen followed by biopsy, so screening would delay her diagnosis. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 14, 18"},{"q":"A metabolic acidosis shows pH 7.48 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 25 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 18 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). What does the carbon dioxide indicate?","choices":["Inadequate respiratory compensation","Appropriate respiratory compensation","Concurrent respiratory acidosis","Concurrent respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Winter formula: 1.5 x 18 + 8 = 35, range 33 to 37. The measured 25 is below the range, so the lungs are removing more carbon dioxide than compensation requires: a concurrent respiratory alkalosis, which also explains the alkaline pH. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man has a first primary spontaneous pneumothorax. On his upright chest radiograph, the apex-to-cupola distance is 4 cm and the interpleural distance at the level of the hilum is 1.5 cm. How is its size classified by the American College of Chest Physicians definition?","choices":["Large, by the hilum distance","Small, by the apex distance","Small, by the hilum distance","Large, by the apex distance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The American College of Chest Physicians sizes a pneumothorax by the apex-to-cupola distance: under 3 cm is small, 3 cm or more is large. His 4 cm apex-to-cupola distance makes it large; the interpleural distance at the hilum is the British guideline measurement. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman has daytime sleepiness, and her partner reports habitual snoring and pauses in her breathing. Her polysomnogram shows 8 predominantly obstructive respiratory events per hour, and her symptoms are not explained by another condition, a medication or a substance. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["Not diagnostic until 15 events per hour","Diagnostic of obstructive sleep apnea","Diagnostic of central sleep apnea","Indeterminate until a home kit is done"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis is confirmed with 5 or more predominantly obstructive events per hour plus a symptom such as sleepiness, or habitual snoring and breathing interruptions noted by a bed partner, when no other condition, medication or substance explains them. She has 8 events and those symptoms. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 9, 8, 26"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman has idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension and is not vasoreactive on testing. She is in functional class II with stable symptoms and no signs of right ventricular failure. Which treatment approach fits her?","choices":["Early oral combination therapy","High-dose oral calcium channel blocker","Continuous intravenous prostanoid","Oral monotherapy, adding drugs later"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients who are not vasoreactive are stratified by risk, and low or intermediate risk (class II or III) calls for oral combination therapy: an endothelin receptor antagonist with a phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitor. Starting the combination early is superior to sequential monotherapy. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 26, 28"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman is being discharged after her primary spontaneous pneumothorax has resolved. She has a flight booked for a conference and asks for advice about air travel. Which advice is accurate?","choices":["Avoid air travel permanently","Fly once the chest pain is gone","Delay flying; confirm with a film","Fly if her oxygen level is normal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Air travel is delayed for 7-14 days after resolution, and resolution is confirmed with a chest X-ray before she flies. Follow-up films verify complete lung re-expansion, and she is also counseled on recurrence and, if she smokes, on stopping. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 59, 60"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has had interstitial lung disease for 8 years, and his right ventricle has been known to be hypertrophied. Over the past month his exertional dyspnea has worsened. Which new finding shows that his right ventricle has decompensated?","choices":["Pink frothy sputum","Flattened diaphragms on the film","Orthopnea with bibasilar crackles","Tender hepatomegaly with ascites"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When a chronically hypertrophied right ventricle decompensates, its elevated diastolic pressure transmits backward: the liver congests and becomes tender, ascites forms and pitting edema spreads in dependent areas. A right ventricular heave, an intensified P2 and a tricuspid regurgitation murmur go with it. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 11, 13, 23, 35"},{"q":"Which wave does the electroencephalogram show in stage N2?","choices":["Sawtooth waves","Delta waves","Beta waves","Theta waves"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Stage N2, the less responsive, deeper sleep that is the longest stage by total duration, shows theta waves, 4 to 7 cycles per second, on the electroencephalogram. Delta waves wait for stage N3. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 25, 28"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and a 65 pack-year history has worsening cough, wheezing and hemoptysis. Computed tomography shows a central mass in a main bronchus with cavitation and a spiculated (spiky) border. Which noninvasive test can sometimes catch this tumor type, unlike the other lung cancers?","choices":["Chest radiograph","Mucicarmine stain","Sputum cytology","Serum calcium level"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A central, cavitating bronchial mass in a heavy smoker with cough, wheezing and hemoptysis is the picture of squamous cell carcinoma, which unlike the other lung cancers can be caught with sputum testing. Sputum cytology is not sensitive in general, so tissue biopsy is still the standard. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 30, 32, 39, 40, 46"},{"q":"Where is the needle placed for needle decompression of a tension pneumothorax?","choices":["Second intercostal space, opposite side","Second intercostal space, midclavicular line","Second intercostal space, midaxillary line","Under the third rib, midclavicular line"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The typical needle decompression site is on the thoracic side of the pneumothorax, in the 2nd intercostal space, over the 3rd rib, at the midclavicular line. Patient body habitus can change the choice, which is why this is called the typical site. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 38, 64, 67, 68"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man with small cell lung carcinoma diagnosed 2 months ago has a dull ache under his right ribs, poor appetite and yellowing of his eyes. Exam shows an enlarged liver with a firm edge. Which complication has developed?","choices":["Adrenal metastasis","Bone metastasis","Liver metastasis","Brain metastasis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Small cell carcinoma is metastatic, to the liver, bone or brain, before detection in over 70% of patients. Liver metastasis causes a dull right upper quadrant ache, hepatomegaly with a firm edge, lack of appetite, jaundice and eventually hepatic failure, matching his findings. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 13, 41"},{"q":"A 63-year-old former shipyard worker undergoes lung biopsy, which shows ferruginous bodies (a histologic result of inorganic dust inhalation) within fibrotic interstitium. What does this finding suggest?","choices":["Active sarcoidosis","Established mesothelioma","Organic dust hypersensitivity","Significant asbestos exposure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A ferruginous body is a histologic finding in interstitial lung disease that results from inorganic dust inhalation, and in tissue it suggests significant asbestos exposure. His years in shipyards are a listed asbestos setting, with insulation, demolition and construction. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 25, 44, 55, 59, 70"},{"q":"An 81-year-old woman with severe heart failure is unfit for surgery. Computed tomography shows a growing 2.5-cm nodule lying against a large blood vessel, and the interventional radiologist judges image-guided biopsy unsafe. Positron emission tomography shows intense uptake in the nodule and no lymph node or distant spread. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Repeat imaging in a year","Image-guided biopsy despite risk","Wedge resection with frozen section","Radiotherapy without biopsy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. For a patient unfit for surgery, image-guided biopsy is the next step only if it is possible and safe. When biopsy is not possible or not safe, the pathway goes straight to stereotactic ablative radiotherapy, radiofrequency ablation or conventional radical radiotherapy, so her unsafe biopsy does not block treatment. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 15, 16, 17"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man has taken amiodarone for atrial fibrillation for 6 years. Over several months he has developed progressive exertional dyspnea, a dry cough and bibasilar inspiratory crackles, and pulmonary function testing shows a restrictive pattern. He has no occupational exposures. How is his interstitial lung disease classified?","choices":["Known cause, occupational exposure","Granulomatous lung disease","Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","Known cause, treatment related"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Amiodarone sits with radiation, methotrexate, nitrofurantoin and chemotherapeutics in the treatment-related group of interstitial lung disease of known cause. His exertional dyspnea, dry cough, crackles and restrictive pattern fit interstitial disease, and his years of amiodarone supply the cause. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 20, 23, 35"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman has a biopsy-proven 2-cm non-small cell carcinoma in the right upper lobe. On exam a firm right supraclavicular lymph node is palpable, and its biopsy shows the same cancer. Imaging shows no metastasis to other organs. Which stage is her cancer?","choices":["Stage 2B","Stage 3A","Stage 3B","Stage 4A"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Spread to any supraclavicular or scalene node is N3, which moves a cancer to stage 3. With a tumor no more than 5 cm, spread to supraclavicular or scalene nodes, or to mediastinal or hilar nodes on the side without the tumor, is stage 3B. Her 2-cm tumor with a positive supraclavicular node fits. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 25, 27, 29"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with known small cell lung carcinoma has a serum sodium of 124 mEq/L (normal 135-145) and low serum osmolality from the syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone. Her family asks why her legs and lungs show no swelling if her body is holding on to too much water. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Her sodium is actually high","She must be dehydrated instead","It develops slowly and insidiously","Swelling will appear within days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In small cell lung cancer, neuroendocrine tumor cells produce excess antidiuretic hormone (vasopressin), and the body retains too much water, lowering serum sodium and osmolality. The result is confusion, seizures, ataxia and other neurologic dysfunction, but no signs of peripheral or pulmonary edema, because the process is insidious over time. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 53-year-old man has loud snoring, witnessed apneas and daytime sleepiness, and his primary care clinician suspects obstructive sleep apnea. Which referral is part of his care?","choices":["Cardiologist","Maxillofacial surgeon","Bariatric surgeon","Sleep physician"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patient education for obstructive sleep apnea includes referral to a sleep physician, together with proper counseling. His snoring, witnessed apneas and sleepiness make the diagnosis likely, and confirming it takes a sleep study. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 15, 13, 18"},{"q":"How is the change in anion gap calculated?","choices":["Calculated anion gap minus 6","Calculated anion gap minus 10","Calculated anion gap minus 12","Calculated anion gap minus 25"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Change in anion gap = calculated anion gap minus a normal gap, with 10 the accepted value. It is then compared with the change in bicarbonate, measured from an accepted normal of 25; the two should be equal in a simple metabolic acidosis. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 27, 50, 7"},{"q":"After a response to initial therapy, which lung cancer is offered prophylactic cranial irradiation?","choices":["Adenocarcinoma","Large cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Small cell carcinoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In small cell carcinoma with a complete or partial response to initial therapy, prophylactic cranial irradiation is recommended in limited stage and considered, with or without thoracic radiotherapy, in extensive stage. Brain magnetic resonance imaging is the preferred scan of the brain. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 33, 35, 40, 43"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has a small primary spontaneous pneumothorax. Her respiratory rate is 20 breaths per minute, heart rate 96 beats per minute, blood pressure 118/74 mm Hg and oxygen saturation 96% on room air, and she speaks in whole sentences. How is she classified by the American College of Chest Physicians stability definition?","choices":["Unstable: heart rate","Stable","Unstable: respiratory rate","Unstable: oxygen saturation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Every criterion is met: respiratory rate 20, under 24; heart rate 96, between 60 and 120; normal blood pressure; room-air saturation 96%, over 90%; and whole sentences. Stability requires all of these, and she has all of them. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has acute bird fancier's lung from the pigeons he keeps at home, and his symptoms are settling. He asks whether he can keep the birds if he takes medicine whenever symptoms flare. Which counsel is accurate?","choices":["Continued exposure may worsen his disease","His lung damage is already permanent","Medicine makes ongoing exposure safe","His symptoms come from asthma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Prompt diagnosis is key so the patient can be removed from the irritating environment: symptoms can be reversible if caught early, but continued exposure may lead to a worsened disease state. Treatment starts with identifying and avoiding the agent, here his birds. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 65, 69, 71"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man has had nasogastric suction for 4 days after bowel surgery and has dry mucous membranes. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.50 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 48 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 36 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). His potassium is 3.0 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L), and his urine chloride is low. Along with stopping the suction, which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Fluid restriction with a diuretic","Saline with potassium replacement","Ventilation to lower his carbon dioxide","A hydrocortisone infusion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Gastric suction causes a saline-responsive metabolic alkalosis, confirmed by the low urine chloride, and he is volume depleted. Treatment is to stop contributors such as suction and treat the fluid and potassium deficits. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 53, 54"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has acute cardiogenic pulmonary edema. He is sitting upright on high-flow oxygen and has received an intravenous loop diuretic, but he remains tachypneic with an oxygen saturation of 87%. He is alert and able to protect his airway. Which step comes next?","choices":["Intravenous fluid bolus","Noninvasive ventilation","Routine intravenous morphine","Lying flat to rest"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Noninvasive positive pressure ventilation is the P in the toolbox: it mechanically stents flooded alveoli open, pushes fluid back across the membrane and lowers intrathoracic preload and afterload. He is alert and can protect his airway, and oxygen with diuresis has not been enough. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 32, 39, 40"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has chronic cor pulmonale. His electrocardiogram shows peaked P waves, severe right axis deviation and a tall R wave in lead V1. Which chamber change does the tall R wave in V1 indicate?","choices":["Left ventricular hypertrophy","Right ventricular hypertrophy","Left atrial enlargement","Right atrial enlargement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A tall R wave in V1 is the electrocardiogram sign of right ventricular hypertrophy, the thickened chamber of chronic cor pulmonale. His peaked P waves point to right atrial enlargement, and severe right axis deviation goes with the chronic disease. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 56-year-old woman has 2 days of mild pleuritic chest pain. Her Wells score is 0, and the clinician's gestalt for pulmonary embolism is very low. Her heart rate is 84/min and oxygen saturation 98%. She has no hemoptysis, takes no estrogen, has no prior clots, has had no surgery or trauma and has no leg swelling. Applying the PERC (Pulmonary Embolism Rule-out Criteria), which step fits?","choices":["Stop: no further testing","Order a D-dimer","Ventilation-perfusion scan","Computed tomographic angiography"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. She fails one criterion, age under 50. A patient with a Wells score of 4 or less who fails any rule-out criterion goes to a D-dimer: a negative result rules embolism out, and a positive one leads to imaging. She passes every other item. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 54, 57"},{"q":"A 57-year-old stone worker has had silicosis for 8 years. He asks which complication his lung disease puts him at increased risk of. Which is it?","choices":["Mesothelioma","Tuberculosis","Goodpasture syndrome","Hypercalcemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Silicosis carries an increased risk of tuberculosis, together with progressive massive fibrosis and scleroderma. That is why patients with silicosis should have a tuberculosis skin test and a current chest film, and why the risk deserves mention at follow-up. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 25, 44, 59, 64"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman wakes in the morning and stays awake through the day without effort. According to the proposed sleep-wake theory, what keeps her wakefulness going once it begins?","choices":["Rising parasympathetic tone","Continuous activation of sleep centers","Reticular-cortex positive feedback","Fatigue of activating-system neurons"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Once wakefulness begins, it can sustain itself naturally because of a positive feedback mechanism: the reticular activating nuclei excite the cerebral cortex and peripheral nervous system, which send positive feedback signals back to the same nuclei and keep them active. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 18, 19, 14"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man had a large anterior myocardial infarction 3 years ago and has heart failure with an ejection fraction of 25%. He now has ankle edema and distended neck veins, and echocardiography shows a dilated right ventricle. Right heart catheterization shows an elevated mean pulmonary artery pressure with a pulmonary capillary wedge pressure of 27 mmHg. How is his right-sided failure classified?","choices":["Group 2 pulmonary hypertension","Group 1 arterial hypertension","Group 4 thromboembolic disease","Cor pulmonale from lung disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A wedge pressure of 27 mmHg shows high left-sided filling pressure, and his failing left ventricle (ejection fraction 25%) is pushing pressure back into the lungs. Left heart disease, including heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, is World Health Organization Group 2, the passive post-capillary group, and Group 2 is not cor pulmonale. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 4, 19"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with acute respiratory distress syndrome from diffuse pneumonia is on low tidal volume ventilation. His arterial oxygen pressure is 80 mm Hg on a fraction of inspired oxygen of 0.50, and an echocardiogram has excluded cardiogenic edema. Which intervention decreases mortality at his severity?","choices":["Transfusion to a higher hemoglobin","Routine pulmonary artery catheter","Early prone positioning","High-frequency oscillatory ventilation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. His ratio is 80 / 0.50 = 160 mm Hg, moderate acute respiratory distress syndrome. Early prone positioning decreases mortality in moderate to severe cases, so he qualifies, alongside the low tidal volume ventilation he is already receiving. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 72, 73, 74"},{"q":"A 38-year-old Black woman has a dry cough and dyspnea of insidious onset. She has lupus pernio (skin sores on the face), nontender lymphadenopathy and liver and spleen enlargement. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","Sarcoidosis","Systemic lupus erythematosus","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lupus pernio, skin sores on the face, is the skin involvement of sarcoidosis, and a dry cough, insidious dyspnea, nontender lymphadenopathy and liver and spleen enlargement complete its multi-organ picture. Sarcoidosis also occurs more often in Black patients and in women. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 18, 25, 35, 39, 40, 42, 43"},{"q":"In interstitial lung disease, what does a positive c-ANCA (cytoplasmic antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody) test suggest?","choices":["Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Collagen vascular disease","Active sarcoidosis","Goodpasture syndrome"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A positive c-ANCA (cytoplasmic antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody) points to granulomatosis with polyangiitis, formerly Wegener's, a granulomatous vasculitis that is one of the interstitial lung diseases with a granulomatous response. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 21, 25, 44"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman who has never smoked has a small, flat nodular opacity lying along the minor fissure on computed tomography done after chest trauma. She asks whether it could be cancer. Which response is accurate?","choices":["It needs removal now","Fissure location signals cancer","Likely scar or lymphoid tissue","It is a hamartoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A nodule close to a fissure usually indicates something benign, and a nodular opacity adjacent to the minor fissure is typical of a linear scar or lymphoid tissue. She is a never-smoker without other risk factors, which also lowers concern. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 16, 21"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with cirrhosis and a low albumin has had watery diarrhea for 6 days. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.34 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 36 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 19 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Serum sodium 136 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 112 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 19 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). His albumin is 2.5 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.5 g/dL). His anion gap calculates to 5. Which step comes next in interpreting the gap?","choices":["Check for bromide intoxication","Search the raised-gap causes","Screen for a plasma cell dyscrasia","Correct the gap for his low albumin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A low gap is most often a low albumin, and the gap must be corrected when albumin is abnormal. Adding 2.5 for each 1 g/dL below 4.5 gives 5 + 5 = 10, a normal gap, which fits a normal-gap metabolic acidosis from his diarrhea. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 8, 46, 48, 57"},{"q":"How many of Light's criteria must a pleural fluid meet to be classified as an exudate?","choices":["Each of the three","At least one","The protein criterion","At least two"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An exudate meets at least one of Light's criteria: pleural-to-serum protein ratio above 0.5, pleural-to-serum lactate dehydrogenase ratio above 0.6, or pleural fluid lactate dehydrogenase above two-thirds of the upper limit of normal serum lactate dehydrogenase. A transudate meets none of them. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 101, 110, 112"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with a 50 pack-year history has a large, cavitated mass in the periphery of the left upper lobe. Weeks later he develops crampy abdominal pain, and imaging shows a metastasis in the small intestine. Biopsy of the lung mass shows undifferentiated cells about three times normal size that do not stain for mucin. Which lung cancer type is most likely?","choices":["Adenocarcinoma","Large cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Small cell carcinoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Large cell carcinoma forms in the periphery, shows a large cavitated mass on computed tomography, and has anaplastic, undifferentiated cells about 3 times larger than baseline that lack specific markers. Negative mucicarmine staining may point to it, and its spread to unusual sites such as the small intestine and bowel is characteristic. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 32, 33, 34, 39, 42"},{"q":"A 56-year-old coal miner has severe complicated coal workers' pneumoconiosis with worsening dyspnea despite oxygen, vaccinations, pulmonary rehabilitation and smoking cessation. Which referral may be indicated?","choices":["Lung transplant evaluation","Thoracic surgery to remove dust","Extracorporeal membrane oxygenation","Yearly ophthalmologic evaluation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Management of coal workers' pneumoconiosis is supportive, with oxygen, vaccinations, rehabilitation and smoking cessation, and in severe cases lung transplant may be indicated. He has severe disease that is worsening despite every supportive measure on the list. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 48, 54, 74"}]};
